Z||000000||EdsB52||08-17-2001||06:27 PM||||I'm not trying to elicit bets via PFF, but who loves to bet on sports? I for one, live for the thrill of it. My largest bet was $1,650 on the Browns in 1989, where the ball hit the upright in overtime and I subsequently lost. But I've won my fair share of dime ($1,000) games. Just curious who else in here like to sports gamble.||||reg||1
Z||000001||JSocha||08-17-2001||06:55 PM||||Just answering this thread could be a gamble.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>But I'm not gambling with my life or my wife.  Heading to the lake for some R&R.  See you all Sunday night or Monday morning.  Wife is just about ready to go and dah truck b runnin!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||maryjane||08-17-2001||07:18 PM||||All my extra cash goes into Wall Street. That's a big enough gamble for me.||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||08-17-2001||07:42 PM||||I was getting beat up by stock market guys at work until a year ago. They would brag and tell me how foolish sports betting was and reinforce their argument with huge gains in the market. They would also say that the stock market want gambling; whatever. Now, they aren't quite so cocky. I like the stock market and play it as well, but it doesn't bring the same level of adrenaline that sports betting does. I about break even with sports betting (minus the juice), but I have lost half my holdings in the market. It just cracks me up when guys try to legitimize Wall Street and illegitimize sports gambling. The similarities are:<P>1) Pay a commission to an agent for the privilege of playing<BR>2) Risk losing everything or doubling, tripling, etc.<P><BR>The differences are:<BR>1) More immediate monetary risk with sports gambling.<BR>2) Learn results of risk (other than futures bets) immediately.||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||08-17-2001||08:07 PM||||Yeah, there's a more immediate monetary gain in betting too.Stocks? It's taken me years(decades really) to get where I am, & it's still all just on paper. I've stuck with energy all this time and avoided .coms, against the many wishes of my brokers.I don't see anything wrong with sports betting, as long as it stays away from the players & coaches. Always felt bad for Pete Rose.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||08-18-2001||12:54 PM||||The .coms <I>had</I> to crash, sooner or later. Their value was <I>wildly</I> inflated.<BR>Having said that, I wish I would have bought Mindspring stock, several years ago. I could tell by how they did business that they were going to go places. Ultimately, they bought Netcom and a few other ISP's, and were themselves bought by Earthlink.<BR>I don't bet on sports. Don't keep up with them, enough.<BR>I don't gamble much at all, really. Might throw $5 towards the Lotto.<BR>Last summer I dropped a couple hundred in the casinos in Gulfport. Didn't expect to win, but you never know. Ended up doing okay, though. Was good entertainment and the alcohol was free. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
